AB  - While the circular economy promises to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and resource consumption, its implementation in industrial practice remains challenging and fragmented. Realizing its full potential requires more than just technical innovations or isolated initiatives. It demands strategic, cross-organizational transformation across the value creation system. Yet, despite increasing enthusiasm in academia, industry, and policy, the barriers to fostering the necessary partnerships, for instance, via cooperations and circular ecosystems, remain insufficiently understood. This study identifies those key barriers through a systematic literature review. These barriers were subsequently structured using interpretive structural modelling, based on expert input gathered during a workshop with practitioners from the manufacturing sector. The workshop enabled a systematic assessment of interdependencies and interactions among the identified barriers.The result is a practice-informed framework contextualizing the issues for establishing and sustaining partnerships for circular value creation in industrial contexts. By offering a differentiated overview of barriers, this article provides theoretical insights into mechanisms for circular partnerships. The findings also offer practical guidance for practitioners seeking to establish and strengthen circular value networks.
