AB  - Hydrogen refueling stations are critical infrastructure for carbon neutrality, yet their safety resilience assessment faces challenges of methodological isolation and insufficient parameter validation. This study proposes an integrated DEMATEL-ISM-BN evaluation model that implements a complete tri-method closed-loop chain with systematic knowledge transfer across causal identification, structural modeling, and probabilistic inference. A 25-factor assessment system spanning resistance, adaptation, and recovery dimensions is constructed. Expert-knowledge-driven analysis of a commercial station in Foshan, China reveals a five-level hierarchical risk transmission mechanism, with maintenance standardization (R43, score = 0.870) identified as the critical hub—confirmed by forward inference showing substantial probability increases in four of six core equipment factors. A risk-sensitivity quadrant classification enables differentiated management strategies. Organizational management factors exhibit high driving force but low sensitivity as root causes, while emergency recovery factors demonstrate the strongest sensitivity (SI = 35.7%) as priority monitoring indicators.
