AB  - The article presents an in-depth analysis of the Entrepreneurship and Innovation Ecosystem (EIE) of Crete, employing the Quintuple Innovation Helix (QIH) framework to explore systemic capabilities and bottlenecks. Drawing on empirical data from 25 regional firms and key innovation actors, the study reveals a science-anchored yet structurally imbalanced ecosystem. While institutions -such as the University of Crete, the Foundation for Research and Technology-Hellas (FORTH) and the Science and Technology Park of Crete (STEP-C)- provide a robust academic base, commercialization pathways, innovation partnerships, and ecosystem coordination remain limited. The analysis uncovers gaps in venture funding, talent specialization, IP strategy, and civil society engagement. By mapping the maturity levels across the five helices—academia, industry, government, civil society, and the environment—the paper identifies strategic asymmetries and proposes two integrated prongs for ecosystem enhancement: structural functionality and firm-level resilience. A six-step roadmap for QIH implementation is outlined to guide regional innovation policy. The findings offer a replicable blueprint for other peripheral ecosystems seeking inclusive, knowledge-driven, and sustainability-aligned innovation growth.
